  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Lend and borrow are often one verb in other languages, or the direction is shown by a particle. In English lend is what the giver does and borrow what the receiver does: a bank lends, a customer borrows. The quality sense (lend weight to) is usually a different verb.",
    "grammar": "Lend takes two patterns: lend someone something or lend something to someone. Pronoun objects take the second: lend it to me. The receiver never takes for. Lend never means take: not I lent a book from the library.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The past tense and past participle are lent, said with a t at the end; there is no form lended."
  },
